# Line-ending policy for content that ends up inside asset bundles.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# core.autocrlf is true on at least one machine here, so git rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout for
# anything it considers text. For source that is harmless. For a TextAsset it is not: the converted
# bytes are the bytes Unity packs into the bundle, so the same commit produces a different bundle
# depending on who checked it out. Observed directly — corpus-asset-c.txt came back from a
# `git checkout` at 175 bytes where the generator had written 173, and the next build emitted a
# differently-hashed group C bundle with nothing in the project actually changed.
#
# That breaks the property the whole Phase 1 measurement rests on: a rebuild with no source change
# must produce byte-identical bundles, or the snapshot diff cannot tell "rebuilt" from "changed",
# and a Linux CI runner and a Windows workstation would disagree about every patch size.
#
# -text tells git to leave the bytes alone in both directions.

# Test corpus: generated with LF by CdnTestContentCLI, must stay exactly as generated.
Assets/Examples/CdnTest/*.txt -text
Assets/Examples/CdnTest/*.png binary

# The same reasoning applies to any future TextAsset that ships in a bundle. Add it here when you
# add it to a group; a bundle whose bytes depend on the checkout is not reproducible.
